 construct:{q:"Whether the will is moved of necessity by the lower appetite?",ref:"ST I-II q.10 a.3",
  model:{obj:"It seems so: 'the good which I will, I do not; but the evil which I hate, that I do' (Rom 7:19); so the will is moved of necessity by concupiscence.",sc:"Augustine says (Retract. i, 9) that it is by the will that we sin; but if moved of necessity, there would be no sin.",resp:"Sometimes reason is wholly bound, as in those who through a violent access of anger or concupiscence become furious or insane, as from some bodily disorder; then the act is not voluntary, as with irrational animals. Sometimes reason is not entirely engrossed, and keeps its judgment free in part; then the will remains free to resist or not. So the will is not moved of necessity by the lower appetite, except where reason is wholly bound.",rep:"Romans 7 speaks of the movements of concupiscence that precede consent, not of consent itself."},
  rubric:["Objection cites Rom 7:19.","Sed contra cites Augustine.","Respondeo: when reason is wholly bound (fury, insanity) the act is not voluntary.","Respondeo: otherwise the will can resist.","Application to mania and degrees of responsibility."]},
 drill:{title:"Order a relapse-prevention plan",parts:["Identify personal early warning signs","Agree who notices and who is called","Protect sleep and routine","Medication review","Brief admission or crisis plan if needed","Debrief and restore relationships afterwards"]},
