 construct:{q:"Whether one person can understand one and the same thing better than another?",ref:"ST I q.85 a.7",
  model:{obj:"It seems not: Augustine says that whoever understands a thing otherwise than as it is does not understand it; so there are no degrees of understanding the same thing.",sc:"Experience shows that one person understands more deeply than another, as one sees more clearly than another.",resp:"'More' can refer to the thing understood (and then one cannot understand the same thing more, since to understand it otherwise is to be deceived), or to the one understanding. In the latter sense one may understand better through a greater power of understanding: first, because the intellect's power follows the disposition of the body to which the soul is allotted; second, because of the lower powers the intellect needs in its operation (imagination, memory, cogitative power), whose better disposition aids understanding.",rep:"Augustine's point concerns the object; differences arise on the side of the knower's powers."},
  rubric:["Objection cites Augustine.","Sed contra from experience.","Respondeo distinguishes object and subject.","Respondeo: bodily disposition and lower powers.","Application: differences in ability do not touch human dignity."]},
 drill:{title:"Order the SIAS support process",parts:["Teacher notices a barrier to learning","Teacher-level support and documentation","School-based support team (SBST) review","Support plan with parents and learner","District-based support team if high support needed","Review and adjust"]},
