Object.assign(EXTRAS,{
 p16:{plain:{version:"Trauma is common in South Africa, but most people recover. PTSD means the memory keeps acting as if the danger is still present. Trauma-focused CBT and EMDR are the best-supported treatments. Forgiveness can be part of healing, but it is not forgetting or excusing, and it should never be forced.",question:"Can trauma be healed, and do I have to forgive?",one:"Yes, with trauma-focused therapy; forgiveness renounces hatred, not truth or justice."},
  hook:"The risen Christ kept his wounds: healed, not erased.",
  analogy:{title:"The filing cabinet",text:"A traumatic memory is like a file never put away: it keeps falling open on the desk. Therapy does not shred the file; it helps file it in the past, where it can be opened deliberately rather than spilling out on its own."},
  sort:{title:"PTSD cluster (ICD-11)",cats:["Re-experiencing","Avoidance","Sense of current threat"],items:[["Flashback during a taxi ride",0],["Avoiding the street where it happened",1],["Startling at every loud sound",2],["Nightmares reliving the event",0],["Refusing to talk about it",1],["Constantly scanning for danger",2]]}},
 p17:{plain:{version:"Bipolar disorder involves episodes of depression and of abnormally high or irritable mood (mania or hypomania). Lithium reduces suicide; therapy with families helps prevent relapse; sleep and routine are crucial. In mania, responsibility can be greatly reduced. Religious experiences during mania need careful, gentle discernment.",question:"Is my high mood a gift from God or an illness?",one:"Look at the fruits, humility, obedience, and the body; seek both clinical and spiritual guidance."},
  hook:"\"When he shall give sleep to his beloved\" (Ps 126:2).",
  analogy:{title:"The thermostat",text:"Mood normally has a thermostat that keeps it within a comfortable range. In bipolar disorder, the thermostat swings too far both ways. Medication, sleep and routine help keep it steady."},
  sort:{title:"Manic religiosity or authentic grace?",cats:["Suggests illness","Suggests grace"],items:[["Grandiose sense of mission, no sleep",0],["Deeper humility and peace",1],["Refuses all counsel",0],["Submits to a spiritual director",1],["Debt and broken relationships",0],["Fidelity to ordinary duties",1]]}},
 p18:{plain:{version:"ADHD is a common, highly heritable condition affecting attention, activity and impulse control. It is not laziness: Thomas already saw that bodily dispositions limit reason's control. Medication works well in the short term; structure, routines and accommodations help a great deal. ADHD distractibility is not the vice of curiosity.",question:"Am I lazy, or do I have ADHD?",one:"Get assessed; ADHD is a bodily disposition, helped by medication, structure and grace."},
  hook:"Augustine and the dog coursing a hare (Confessions X.35).",
  analogy:{title:"A powerful engine, weak brakes",text:"Barkley likens ADHD to a car with a strong engine and weak brakes. The driver is not bad; the car needs better brakes (medication, structure) and safer roads (fewer distractions)."},
  sort:{title:"Inattention or hyperactivity–impulsivity?",cats:["Inattention","Hyperactivity–impulsivity"],items:[["Loses keys and notes often",0],["Interrupts others",1],["Can't sustain attention in lectures",0],["Fidgets constantly",1],["Forgets deadlines",0],["Blurts out answers",1]]}},
 p19:{plain:{version:"Autism affects social communication and involves restricted or repetitive behaviours and sensory differences. About 1 in 100 children are diagnosed worldwide. In South Africa, inclusive-education policy aims to support autistic learners in ordinary schools where possible. Supports should respect the person's dignity, not just aim to make them look 'normal'.",question:"How can schools and parishes best support autistic children?",one:"Predictability, clear communication, sensory care, using interests, and full inclusion."},
  hook:"\"Those that seem to be the more feeble members of the body are more necessary\" (1 Cor 12:22).",
  analogy:{title:"Different operating system",text:"Many autistic people describe their minds as running a different operating system: not broken, but incompatible with some 'software' designed for others. Good support adapts the environment as well as teaching new skills."},
  sort:{title:"Which support area?",cats:["Predictability","Communication","Sensory","Social"],items:[["Visual timetable",0],["AAC tablet",1],["Ear defenders",2],["Structured peer games",3],["Transition warnings",0],["Quiet corner",2]]}}
});
